Z||000002||Vader||06-02-2001||11:20 PM||||Drew,<P>The only "bad tiem" to change to synthetic engine oil is when the engine is leaking/consuming oil so badly that it is economically ineffective to use synthetics.  <P>One thing to remember about synthetics is that you should not intermix brands.  Mobil 1 is a PE (polyol ester) base and some other brands are PAO (polyalpha olefin) based.  Get an oil and stick with it.  Since GM loads its engines with Mobil 1 from the factory, I use it exclusively.  Mobil also has the longest/most experience with synthetis, beginning in the 1970's in industrial applications (if you think $20.00 is too expensive for an engine oil change with synthetics, try $3,300.00 for a 200HP air compressor).  <P>In a pinch you can mix a mineral oil with synthetic without any adverse reactions, but intermixing types of synthetics can cause solids to precipitate out of the oil, gumming and sludge formation, and ineffective lubrication.  <P>Also, since synthetis are a lot more stable, you don't need to use the lower viscosity oils to insure low temperature protection and lubrication.  10W synthetic will pump and lubricate effectively down to -54°F, whereas 5W mineral oil gives up at -30°F.  I NEVER use the 0W- or 5W synthetics since it is unnecessary and sacrifices higher temperature protection.  <P>It's a different game with synthetics, but if you follow the rules you can win the game.  I tore down an LT1 with 110,000 miles on synthetic for a cam change/head and valve customization/general refresh.  The WORST bore I measured had 0.0004" of taper/out-of round.  Not four thousandths, four <B>tenths</B>.  Basically, the engine had jsut reached the limit of factory production tolerances, and was not anywhere near the service tolerances.  Crank rods and mains were the same as the day they were assembled.  I've got photos of the non-existent ridge and inside mic measurements.  As you might imagine, I'm a believer in synthetic engine oils.<P>------------------<BR>"<I>Let the bodies hit the floor!</I>"||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||06-02-2001||11:39 PM||||Yeah!||||reg||12
Z||000004||XDrewX||06-02-2001||11:45 PM||||I didnt know there were differences in synthetic oil. Thanks for the info.  I have used only mobil 1 synthetic in my 99 grand prix,.. once it got to the 5,000 mile mark.  I was told you should break the engine in with standard oil, so everything will seal better.  the only reason i asked the question is because a buddy of mine started using synthetic oil in his 100,000 mile 454 and a week after he did the change he blew a gasket, i forget which one, and the shop he took it to said he should not have started using synthetic on an engine with that many miles.  ||||reg||1
